6. Orchestration Responsibilities

The orchestration layer SHALL:

Start workflows.
Schedule cognitive tasks.
Invoke services.
Route engineering artifacts.
Synchronize parallel execution.
Coordinate retries.
Pause and resume workflows.
Escalate to human reviewers.
Record execution history.
7. Execution Modes

Atlas supports multiple execution modes.

Sequential

Stages execute one after another.

Suitable for deterministic workflows.

Parallel

Independent stages execute concurrently.

Example:

Evidence
   │
   ├────► Memory
   │
   └────► Knowledge Update
Event-Driven

Execution begins when a triggering event occurs.

Examples:

New sensor reading.
CAD model updated.
User request submitted.
Human-in-the-Loop

Execution pauses for human review or approval before continuing.

Examples:

Regulatory approval.
Design validation.
Safety review.
Continuous

Workflows remain active and respond continuously to changing engineering conditions.

Examples:

Digital twin monitoring.
Structural health assessment.
Construction progress tracking.
8. Workflow States

Every workflow SHALL progress through defined states.

Created
    │
Queued
    │
Running
    │
Paused
    │
Waiting
    │
Completed

or

Failed

or

Cancelled

Each state transition SHALL be recorded for auditability.

9. Coordination Rules

The orchestration layer SHALL ensure:

Dependencies are satisfied before execution.
Required inputs are available.
Context accompanies all tasks.
Constraints are enforced.
Failures are isolated.
Partial results remain traceable.
10. Recovery Model

When failures occur, Atlas MAY:

Retry execution.
Roll back to the previous stable state.
Skip optional tasks.
Request human intervention.
Terminate the workflow.

Recovery actions SHALL preserve engineering integrity and traceability.

11. Human Interaction

The orchestration model SHALL support explicit human interaction points.

Examples include:

Approval requests.
Additional information requests.
Conflict resolution.
Manual overrides.
Decision confirmation.

Human actions SHALL become part of the engineering trace.

12. Conformance

An implementation conforms if it:

Preserves the canonical cognitive pipeline.
Coordinates execution without altering cognitive semantics.
Maintains workflow state.
Preserves traceability.
Supports defined execution modes.
13. Future Evolution

Future revisions MAY introduce:

Multi-agent orchestration.
Distributed workflow coordination.
Adaptive scheduling.
Policy-driven orchestration.
Autonomous workflow optimization.

Such enhancements SHALL preserve the canonical cognitive responsibilities established by ACC and AHS.